Chapter: CollateOrder
  cites Foreword chapter Collate
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 So every letter pair below is printed twice: once through text-collate
 and once through text-compare. The pairs are chosen so the two disagree.
 CCE ranks the letters by English frequency, so in encoding order d comes
 before b and t before a; alphabetically they do not. A run where both
 columns agreed everywhere would mean the key was the identity.

 We say:

Section: Reading

  co : Text, Text -> Text
  co (a) (b) = show (text-collate a b)

  en : Text, Text -> Text
  en (a) (b) = show (text-compare a b)

  pair : Text, Text -> Text
  pair (a) (b) = a & "/" & b & "=" & co a b & "|" & en a b

Section: The table agrees with the encoding it describes

 collate-alpha-index restates the CCE letter order, and a table that
 restates an encoding drifts away from it. to-unicode is the mapping CCE
 publishes; every entry is checked against it here, one character at a
 time, which a test can afford and a comparison loop cannot. The
 lowercase band starts at 13 and the uppercase band at 39 carries the
 same letters in the same order, so both are asked.

  check-band : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  check-band (base) (ascii) (i) (n) (bad) =
    if i >= n then bad
    else let want = to-unicode (base + i) - ascii
    in let got = list-at collate-alpha-index i
    in check-band base ascii (i + 1) n (bad + (if got == want then 0 else 1))

  line-table : Text =
    "table vs to-unicode: lower-mismatches=" & show (check-band 13 97 0 26 0)
    & " upper-mismatches=" & show (check-band 39 65 0 26 0)

  line-accents : Text =
    "accent table: entries=" & show (list-length collate-accent-base)
    & " forms=" & show (112 - 97 + 1)

Section: Letters, both orders side by side

  line-letters : Text =
    "letters: " & pair "a" "b" & " " & pair "b" "c" & " " & pair "c" "d"
    & " " & pair "e" "c" & " " & pair "t" "a" & " " & pair "z" "a"

  line-words : Text =
    "words: " & pair "apple" "banana" & " " & pair "banana" "cherry"
    & " " & pair "cherry" "apple" & " " & pair "apple" "apple"

  line-prefix : Text =
    "prefix: " & pair "app" "apple" & " " & pair "apple" "app"
    & " " & pair "" "a"

Section: Case is a tie-break and not a band

 The discriminating pair here is not the obvious one. CCE's lowercase
 band (13-38) sits entirely BELOW the uppercase band (39-64), so encoding
 order already puts every lowercase word before every uppercase one and
 `apple` before `Banana` agrees in both orders, proving nothing.

 What separates them is a LATE lowercase letter against an EARLY
 uppercase one. `z` is 38 and `A` is 41, so encoding order puts `zebra`
 before `Apple`; alphabetically it comes after. That pair can fail and
 the obvious one cannot.

  line-case : Text =
    "case: " & pair "zebra" "Apple" & " " & pair "apple" "Banana"
    & " " & pair "Apple" "apple" & " " & pair "APPLE" "apple"

Section: The other classes

  line-classes : Text =
    "classes: " & pair "9" "a" & " " & pair "1" "9" & " " & pair "!" "1"
    & " " & pair " " "a"

 The accented character is built from its code point rather than written
 as a literal, so this file stays ASCII on disk. CCE 97 is e-acute, the
 first of the sixteen accented forms.

  e-acute : Text = collate-char 97

  line-accented : Text =
    "accented: e-vs-z=" & co "e" "z" & " base-before-accent=" & co "e" e-acute
    & " accent-before-f=" & co e-acute "f"
    & " encoding-put-it-after-z=" & en e-acute "z"

Section: A sort, which is the point of a key

  seeded : List Text
  seeded = ["delta", "Bravo", "echo", "alpha", "charlie"]

  keyed : List Text, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  keyed (xs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let k = collate-key (list-at xs i)
    in keyed xs (i + 1) n (list-push acc k)

 A selection sort over the keys. A word already emitted has its key
 replaced by a sentinel that outranks every real one: the largest primary
 weight this chapter issues is 100, so a key of a single character 127 is
 above anything collate-key can build.

  pick-min : List Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pick-min (ks) (i) (n) (best) =
    if i >= n then best
    else let ord = text-compare (list-at ks i) (list-at ks best)
    in pick-min ks (i + 1) n (if ord < 0 then i else best)

  sort-run : List Text, List Text, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  sort-run (xs) (ks) (done) (n) (out) =
    if done >= n then out
    else let m = pick-min ks 0 n 0
    in let word = list-at xs m
    in let spent = list-set-at ks m (collate-char 127)
    in let sep = if done > 0 then " " else ""
    in sort-run xs spent (done + 1) n (out & sep & word)

  line-sort : Text =
    let n = list-length seeded
    in "sorted: " & sort-run seeded (keyed seeded 0 n []) 0 n ""

Section: Body

  report-a : Text =
    line-table & "\n" & line-accents & "\n" & line-letters

  report-b : Text =
    line-words & "\n" & line-prefix & "\n" & line-case

  report-c : Text =
    line-classes & "\n" & line-accented & "\n" & line-sort

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni report-a
    print-line-uni report-b
    print-line-uni report-c
  end
